666 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1473 0 R
/E (Mechanical damage from shock and vibration: Poor road conditions, overloading and rough handling expose produce to impact, compression and vibration. These cause bruising, cracking and internal tissue damage that may not be visible at delivery but trigger rapid decay later. Accelerometer-based monitoring \(g-force spikes, vibration frequency\) can identify when and where damage occurred along the route.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [34]
>>
endobj
667 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1474 0 R
/E (Ethylene accumulation in closed transport: Climacteric fruits such as banana, mango and tomato release ethylene, a ripening hormone that accumulates in closed containers. Elevated ethylene causes premature ripening, softening and yellowing, and mixed loads spread it to ethylene-sensitive produce. Monitoring ethylene concentration enables timely ventilation, segregation or use of ethylene scrubbers.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [35]
>>
endobj
668 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1475 0 R
/E (IoT-based cold-chain monitoring limitations: IoT sensor nodes using protocols such as MQTT, LoRa and NB-IoT/LTE-M enable real-time condition monitoring with low bandwidth and power. However, rural transit routes have frequent cellular dropouts, causing data loss or gaps. Research shows the need for edge storage with store-and-forward synchronization, energy-efficient duty cycling and reliable timestamping using GNSS or RTC during connectivity loss.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [36]
>>
endobj
669 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1476 0 R
/E (Blockchain for agri-food traceability: Blockchain provides a decentralized, immutable, append-only ledger where each block is cryptographically linked using hash functions such as SHA-256, making historical records tamper-evident. Frameworks like AgriBlockIoT and Hyperledger Fabric-based supply chains show improved transparency and trust among farmers, transporters, retailers and regulators. Storing only Merkle roots on-chain, while keeping raw data off-chain, improves scalability and reduces transaction cost and latency.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [37]
>>
endobj
670 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1477 0 R
/E (The oracle problem in blockchain-IoT systems: Blockchain guarantees that data cannot be changed after it is written, but it cannot verify whether the data entering it was genuine. This is known as the oracle problem. If sensor data is manipulated at the device, during offline storage or in transmission before reaching the ledger, blockchain only preserves falsified records. Research highlights the need for device-level security such as hash chaining at the edge, sequential record IDs, device authentication \(HMAC/ECDSA\).)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [38]
>>
endobj
